  "knowledgePattern": "Find the similarity scale of repeated altitudes and sum their GP",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Find the first perpendicular",
      "body": "<p>Draw the perpendicular from the right-angle vertex to the hypotenuse and label its height. Express the triangle’s area using its two perpendicular sides, then using the hypotenuse and this height.</p>",
      "recap": "The area of a triangle is half its base times its perpendicular height.",
      "pitfall": "Use the perpendicular to the hypotenuse as the new height.",
      "pause": "Equate the two area expressions.",
      "recall": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Find the length scale",
      "body": "<p>Mark the equal acute angles in the two small triangles. Outline the larger triangle selected for the next step and match its hypotenuse to the corresponding side of the original.</p>",
      "recap": "Corresponding lengths in similar triangles share one scale factor.",
      "pitfall": "Use the larger triangle at every stage.",
      "pause": "Find the ratio from one selected triangle to the next.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-similar-triangles",
          "reminder": "Corresponding lengths in similar triangles share one scale factor."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Sum the perpendiculars",
      "body": "<p>The perpendicular heights shrink by the same length ratio as the triangles. Write their first term and common ratio, then use the sum to infinity.</p>",
      "recap": "An infinite GP has a finite sum when the absolute value of its ratio is less than 1.",
      "pitfall": "A length ratio is not an area ratio.",
      "pause": "Evaluate the sum of the heights.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l09",
          "reminder": "An infinite GP has a finite sum when the absolute value of its ratio is less than 1."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The original triangle has area <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow></math>. Its altitude to the hypotenuse is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> The two triangles made by that altitude are each similar to the original by their angle pairs. The larger has hypotenuse 12, so its length scale relative to the original is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The same construction on the larger triangle repeats this scale. Hence the drawn altitudes form a GP with first term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></math> and ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> The sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow></math>, answer A. The ratio is strictly below 1, so the sum converges; using the smaller subtriangle would give a different ratio and would not follow the instruction.</p>"
}
